% ============================================================================
% precipitation-freeze-threshold — for freezing rain, the NUMERIC freeze
% threshold in degrees Fahrenheit an already-cited NWS sentence states,
% decoded as its own row (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `precipitation-types.adj`
% (`precip_form(precip, form)`, ONE defining physical form per precipitation
% type — freezing_rain → glaze_of_ice). That table's own header already
% quotes, verbatim, an NWS sentence stating the temperature at or below
% which freezing rain refreezes on contact — a numeric figure the single
% `form` atom had no room for:
%
%   freezing_rain:
%     "...they instantly refreeze upon contact with anything that that is
%      at or below freezing (32 degrees F), creating a glaze of ice."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? precipitation_freeze_threshold_f(freezing_rain, $TemperatureF)
%       % 32
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `precipitation_freeze_threshold_f(precip,
% temperature_f)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the
% ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the threshold WITH its
% source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% precipitation type whose own cited span states no numeric freeze
% threshold. `rain`, `snow`, `sleet`, and `hail` are deliberately NOT rows
% here: none of their own already-quoted spans (in
% `precipitation-types.adj`'s header) states a temperature figure -- so
% this sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than its parent, grounding exactly the
% one precipitation type whose cited span states a numeric threshold and
% abstaining on any other, rather than inventing one. The query also runs
% BACKWARD -- bind a temperature and recall which precipitation type it
% names:
%
%     ? precipitation_freeze_threshold_f($Precip, 32)
%       % freezing_rain
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NWS sentence
% `precipitation-types.adj`'s own header already quotes in full for
% freezing_rain -- no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the
% numeric clause as its own row instead of folding the whole sentence into
% one compound `form` atom. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier
% `precipitation-types.adj` already carries (NOAA NWS, a primary U.S.
% government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table precipitation_freeze_threshold_f {
    columns precip, temperature_f

    row (freezing_rain, 32)

    source "...they instantly refreeze upon contact with anything that that is at or below freezing (32 degrees F), creating a glaze of ice."
    locator "https://www.weather.gov/iwx/sleetvsfreezingrain"
    trust authoritative
}
